{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfVAHLBtWamAKAryXamGi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The bootstrap simulator underestimates majority voting since repeated wrong answers inflate its error, giving 0.41 versus the stated 0.51 on dev — it's still useful as a relative comparison tool but I need to account for that bias, or just design the policy directly and validate with `lab evaluate`. I'm starting to sketch the policy by category, noting that easy topics like algebra and number theory have a verifier AUC of 1.0.\n\nFor easy questions, since correct scores tend to sit around 0.06+ and wrong ones cap near 0.35, I'll sample variants sequentially and stop early once a sample clears a threshold, otherwise keep sampling and take the max score at the end, capping around 6 samples per question — expecting roughly 2.5 samples per easy question on average, which leaves the bulk of the budget for the harder questions (~9.5 samples each across 200 hard problems). For hard topics like geometry and combinatorics, the verifier is less reliable: wrong answers can score either very low or misleadingly high depending on the variant, while correct answers tend to cluster in a mid-to-low range and appear consistently across multiple variants — so I'm thinking about weighting agreement across variants rather than trusting raw score alone, treating very low scores as a strong signal of a wrong answer.\n\nI want to test a variant-diversity voting scheme: sample once per variant across all 5, repeat, then among answers scoring above some threshold, weight by how many distinct variants produced that same answer rather than raw count, since wrong answers tend to cluster within a single variant while correct answers reappear across variants — breaking ties by count then mean score. I should check this against plain majority vote and max-score selection using real per-variant samples (up to 4 per variant, 20 total per question) to avoid needing to bootstrap, and also test an early-stopping rule where if an answer already appears in 3 or more distinct variants after just 5 samples, I stop there. I'll set up these strategies and evaluate them against the hard-topic data at different budget levels.\n\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":93,"cache_read_input_tokens":39687,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":93,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c760052f-d682-4c72-8443-e931ccc9c398","uuid":"8653acff-23c6-409c-babb-82345ad1555a","timestamp":"2026-09-28T03:54:38.511Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfVAHLBtWamAKAryXamGi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The offline baseline is lower than the real dev number because of the small per-template pools, so I'll use the simulator for relative comparisons only. Next: test candidate rules for the hard topics directly on the labelled data."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":93,"cache_read_input_tokens":39687,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":93,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c760052f-d682-4c72-8443-e931ccc9c398","uuid":"06f1b080-5344-4ea2-9f36-1b1f8f533c7f","timestamp":"2026-09-28T03:54:38.513Z"}
